--- A function handed as a value, called through it with an argument that can fail in one
--- function (`before[maybe[a], b]`) and with plain values in another (`before[a, b]`).
--- Interpreted: prints `true` twice (the failure would be handed on before the call, as a
--- direct call hands it).
--- Compiled: the program does not build —
---     error[E0308]: mismatched types --> src/main.rs:46:19
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what Crow
---     accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program
--- The generated Rust types the function itself by the fallible call: `fn less(a:
--- CrowResult<i64>, b: i64)`, and every function value of it as `fn(CrowResult<i64>, i64)`,
--- so `values_less`'s `before(a, b)` with a plain i64 does not build. Expected: a parameter
--- is never a value that can fail (spec, Auto-Propagation) — the call through the value
--- hands the failure on before calling, `less` takes `i64`, and both calls build.
--- (With only the fallible call the program builds and agrees, but `less` still takes a
--- CrowResult.) Found writing rosetta/stream_merge.co: a heap entry's line is a record field
--- holding a value that can fail (a source read through `shell`), compared through the
--- merge's order function, which the two-way merge calls with plain lines.

fn maybe[n]
    if n lt 0
        return error['negative', 'demo']
    return n

fn less[a, b]
    return a lt b

fn failing_less[a, b, before]
    return before[maybe[a], b]

fn values_less[a, b, before]
    return before[a, b]

log[failing_less[1, 2, less]]
log[values_less[1, 2, less]]
